# /etc/nftables.d/pi-egress.nft (deployed by remote-deploy.sh)
#
# Egress firewall for uid 1000 — the `sandbox` user the AI's pi
# subprocess + bash subprocesses run as in the dual-uid model
# (pi-typst-test v1.1).
#
# Why uid 1000 instead of uid 1100:
#   The backend (FastAPI) still runs as uid 1100 and DOES need to
#   reach the GCE metadata server so the Vertex SDK can fetch ADC
#   tokens for `roles/aiplatform.user`. Firewalling 1100 broke that
#   path. The AI's effective identity is now uid 1000 (set by the
#   `setpriv --reuid 1000` wrapper in services/pi_session.py), so
#   the firewall can be tightened around exactly that uid without
#   breaking legitimate backend egress.
#
# Closes:
#   1. GCP metadata SSRF (169.254.169.254). Without this rule a
#      prompt that bypasses services/policy.py's regex (sslip.io
#      DNS, decimal IPs, Python urllib bypass, etc.) can hit the
#      metadata server and steal a Vertex IAM token.
#   2. AWS / ECS metadata equivalents (169.254.170.2).
#   3. IPv6 link-local (fe80::/10).
#
# We DO NOT block general egress — pip/npm/git over HTTPS still
# work. uid 1000 in the container == uid 1000 on the host (no user
# namespace), so `skuid 1000` matches the real process uid.
#
# IMPORTANT: this rule must reflect the SANDBOX uid, not the
# backend uid. If you ever change PI_SANDBOX_UID in
# Dockerfile.backend or services/sandbox_layout.py, update the
# `skuid` value here in lockstep.

table inet pi_egress {
    chain output {
        type filter hook output priority filter; policy accept;

        # AWS / ECS metadata (cross-cloud belt-and-suspenders). We are
        # NOT on AWS today, so blocking 169.254.170.2 has zero
        # collateral risk and does still close the cross-cloud SSRF
        # path if we ever migrate.
        skuid 1000 ip daddr 169.254.170.2 drop

        # IPv6 link-local — also unused on our network and a known
        # carrier for cloud-metadata-style services. Keep it blocked.
        skuid 1000 ip6 daddr fe80::/10 drop

        # ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        # NOTE: GCE metadata (169.254.169.254 / 169.254.0.0/16 IPv4)
        # is INTENTIONALLY ALLOWED for uid 1000.
        #
        # The dual-uid sandbox runs both legitimate `pi` and the AI's
        # bash subprocesses as uid 1000 — we cannot differentiate
        # between them at the kernel firewall layer (skuid is process-
        # uid, not process-identity). Blocking 169.254.169.254 here
        # blocks pi's own ADC token fetch alongside any rogue
        # `curl http://metadata.google.internal/...` from the AI's bash.
        # Without ADC, every `--provider anthropic-vertex` /
        # `--provider google-vertex` chat dies at startup with
        # "No API key found", which is what shipped to prod and
        # immediately broke chat for every user.
        #
        # The trust boundary for the metadata token has therefore moved
        # from "kernel firewall" to "GCP IAM scope on the VM service
        # account". As long as the SA only has roles/aiplatform.user
        # (and NOT roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator,
        # roles/storage.admin, roles/owner, etc.), a stolen token can
        # at most spend a small amount of Vertex / Anthropic quota in
        # our project — same blast radius as a paid pi prompt would
        # already have. See deploy/HARDENING.md §6 (GCP IAM bindings)
        # for the SA hardening checklist.
        # ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    }
}
